utt u x t - lap u x t = ((∑ j, ‖ux u j x t‖^2) - ‖ut u x t‖^2) • u (x,t)", "explanation": "At positive time obtain ordinary time and spatial derivatives from ContDiffOn on the open domain. Differentiate the finite component identity ‖u‖²=1 once and twice using product rules (or the real inner-product derivative rule). Dot the pointwise normality equation first with ut and then with u. The scalar is selected only at that point; derive its conditional universal formula without differentiating it, and substitute back.", "source_steps": [{"id": "S001", "text": "Fix x∈V and r>0. H002 supplies continuous derivatives of every order near (x,r). H004 implies u·u=1. For the time-curve applications, extend u and its positive-time derivatives by zero at nonpositive times; these extensions agree with the original functions near every r>0. Apply M001 to the square of each real component of this total time curve and add the m derivatives. Differentiating this constant identity gives 2sum_{k=1}^m u_k (u_t)_k=2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. Since the time and point were arbitrary this identity holds on the entire open domain."}, {"id": "S002", "text": "Choose the pointwise scalar a using H005. Dot u_tt−Δu=a u with u_t. By F001 and symmetry of the real dot product, u_t·(u_tt−Δu)=a(u_t·u)=0. This uses only the value of a at the chosen point, with no differentiation or regularity of a."}, {"id": "S003", "text": "Differentiate the identity in F001 in time, using M001 on each component product and H002 to supply both derivatives: |u_t|^2+u·u_tt=0. For a spatial index j, differentiating u·u=1 in x_j by M001 first gives u·u_j=0 at every point. Differentiate that identity once more in x_j, again by M001, to obtain |u_j|^2+u·u_jj=0. These finite component sums establish u·u_tt=−|u_t|^2 and u·u_jj=−|u_j|^2 for every j."}, {"id": "S004", "text": "Dot the chosen normality equation with u. H004 gives |u|^2=1; F003 and Δu=sum_j u_jj therefore give a=u·(u_tt−Δu)=sum_j|u_j|^2−|u_t|^2. The same calculation applies to any real scalar satisfying that equation, hence gives the claimed universally conditional scalar formula. Substituting it back gives the explicit wave-map equation. F001, F002 and F003 give all remaining clauses. Generalize over x and r; the existentially selected scalar does not escape."}], "boundary": "Only listed inputs are project premises. The pinned Mathlib environment is freely available; no sibling proof is supplied.", "background_supplement_sha256": "578fb631697ce5e2ce4df4eb5e0cbc6a81f5afa3f94df93d7d6b11190e8816a4", "objects": [], "representations": [], "bridges": [], "shared_inputs": [], "background_note_index": [{"id": "R001.BG001", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "kind": "mathlib", "fact": "For every real tuple v in W, |v|^2=sum_{k=1}^m v_k^2=v·v. In particular H004 implies u·u=1. This same norm-square identification applies to the derivative vectors in S003 and to u in S004.", "declaration": "Real.sq_sqrt"}, {"id": "R001.BG002", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "kind": "mathlib", "fact": "For a one-variable real tuple curve with vector derivative d, its kth real component has scalar derivative d_k. In the curves used by A001-A004, the relevant components of the vector derivatives are (u_t)_k, (u_tt)_k, (u_j)_k, and (u_jj)_k, respectively.", "declaration": "hasDerivAt_pi"}, {"id": "R001.BG003", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "kind": "mathlib", "fact": "For a finite set of real scalar curves each having a specified derivative at a real point, their sum has derivative the sum of those derivatives. This licenses adding the m component derivatives in S001 and, with the corresponding product derivatives, in S003; it also covers m=0.", "declaration": "HasDerivAt.fun_sum"}, {"id": "R001.BG004", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "kind": "mathlib", "fact": "Every constant real curve c has derivative zero at every real evaluation point. In the displayed differentiations the constants used are 1 and 0.", "declaration": "hasDerivAt_const"}, {"id": "R001.BG005", "at": "/inference/argument/0/text", "kind": "mathlib", "fact": "Derivative statements are invariant under equality of curves on a neighborhood of the evaluation point. Thus the specified zero extensions of time curves preserve derivatives at r>0, and local equality to a constant permits transfer of its derivative. Spatial coordinate curves at fixed positive r remain in the domain for every real coordinate value.", "declaration": "HasDerivAt.congr_of_eventuallyEq"}, {"id": "R001.BG006", "at": "/inference/argument/2/text", "kind": "mathlib", "fact": "For a real scalar curve, any two derivatives at the same point are equal. Thus the product-rule finite-sum derivative expressions in S001 and S003 equal zero when the differentiated curve is locally the constant 1 or 0.", "declaration": "HasDerivAt.unique"}]}}
